mer).\n\nI cannot resolve A vs B vs C from the repo. This is the single most consequential open question and I want it answered by the researcher/source before the final design.\n\n**2b. Dose the factor on ETTm1's real periodicity scale.** ETTm1 is 10-minute sampled → intrinsic daily cycle = **144 steps**, weekly = **1008 steps**. Candidate levels (to be trimmed to ~7–9 in the final design, expressed in raw timesteps and converted to patch units if mechanism A):\n- `none` (no periodic prior / plain absolute or learned positions) — the reference point\n- harmonics/mistunes of the daily cycle: 12, 24, 48, 72, 96\n- **144 (daily, expected optimum)** and 216/288\n- 504, **1008 (weekly)**\n- `auto/fft` (period estimated from train split, if the model supports it)\nDeliberately include the *plausible-but-wrong* value 24 (correct for hourly ETTh, off by 6× for 10-minute ETTm1). If period genuinely matters, mis-tuned periods should degrade in a smooth, interpretable way; that is the mechanistic signature we test for, rather than just \"which number wins\".\n\n**2c. Baselines / controls.**\n1. `none` (no periodic prior) — primary control, isolates the effect of having a period at all.\n2. 144 / 1008 \"oracle\" periods from ETTm1's known sampling rate.\n3. Mistranslated-period control (24, and a non-divisor like 100) — tests sensitivity/robustness, not just fit.\n4. Lookback-value (seasonal naive) and, if cheap, a simple decomposition baseline (e.g. DLinear) to anchor absolute numbers and tell us whether period effects exceed baseline noise.\n5. PatchTST-without-period ablation of the same backbone if PDF's backbone is patch-based (isolates period from other PDF novelties).\n\n**2d. Data protocol (to be pinned from source, per the task's stated 12/2/… convention).** ETTm1 standard Informer/Autoformer-family split: train = first 12 months, val = next 2 months, test = remainder (~last 3 months), with val/test borders shifted left by `seq_len` so targets exist, borders clamped to the actual file length (~69,680 rows, 7 channels). I will not assert exact integer borders until I see a dataloader; different forks use different constants.\n\n**2e. Horizons / lookback / metrics.** Horizons 96/192/336/720 (one model per horizon, the standard convention). Lookback `seq_len` = 96 as primary (Informer/Autoformer convention) with 336 as a secondary sensitivity arm (PatchTST convention) — flag: with `seq_len=96 < 144`, levels *above* the lookback are mechanically degenerate under mechanism A, which must be handled explicitly, not silently. Metrics: MSE and MAE on train-split-standardized data (scaler fit on train only), averaged over test samples and all 7 channels, reported **per horizon** as well as averaged, plus the relative change vs the `none` control.\n\n**2f. Seeds/repeats.** ≥3 seeds (5 if the grid is trimmed) for every cell, mean ± std, and a pre-registered decision rule: a period effect is claimed only if the gap between the best and `none` exceeds the seed-to-seed std of both cells. Note honestly that most published ETT tables are single-seed, so our own variance estimate is what makes the claim defensible.\n\n**2g. Confound policy.** Main experiment: fix everything else at one declared setting and vary only `period`. Because a fixed patch_len can make some period values unrepresentable (mechanism A), the grid is declared as \"period values rounded to the nearest representable multiple of stride\" with the un-rounded target and actual value both logged. Secondary (small) 2-factor sweep: period × patch_len {8, 12, 16} at horizon 720 only, to show whether period effects are confounded with tokenization. Also pre-declare that hyperparameter selection uses the **val** split only, and report both (i) the effect curve at fixed shared settings and (ii) the best-on-val period per horizon, since these answer slightly different questions.\n\n**2h. Feasibility (stated, not glossed).** As delivered here, **nothing can be executed**: no torch/numpy, n