d is independent-ish of patch_len. UNVERIFIED; if mechanism A (patch-position embedding) holds, period and patch_len/stride are entangled and the grid must be re-expressed in patch units.", "Mechanism ambiguity A/B/C is unresolved and is treated as an open question, not as an assumption already settled.", "ETTm1 = the standard 7-channel 10-minute file with the 12-month train / 2-month val / remaining test convention, borders shifted by seq_len, targets standardized with train-fit scaler. Convention asserted from prior literature, not from repo code.", "Multivariate forecasting over all 7 channels with MSE/MAE averaged across channels; 4 horizons 96/192/336/720, one model trained per horizon; primary seq_len 96 (secondary 336).", "All non-period hyperparameters are fixed at one declared configuration for the main sweep; a small period × patch_len sweep handles the confound separately. If the researcher prefers per-cell co-tuning, the design must be restructured (and the answer becomes 'best-achievable vs period', not 'effect of period').", "≥3 seeds per cell with a pre-registered significance rule; published single-seed ETT tables are not treated as a reliable effect-size reference.", "A code patch may be required if PDF auto-derives period from data and exposes no override. In that case the 'period' factor becomes an intervention on the estimator, not a config knob.", "No experiment is run or claimed in this deliverable; feasibility numbers are planning estimates only."], "uncertainties": ["Identity of 'PDF' and its architecture — completely unknown from the provided material; the whole design hinges on it.", "Exact semantics of 'period' (patch-position period vs. seasonal/decomposition cycle vs. patch length itself) and its units; also whether it must divide the lookback, be a multiple of stride, or be capped by seq_len.", "Whether period can be disabled at all (needed for the `none` control) or whether the control must be implemented as a code ablation.", "Whether seq_len=96 (standard) is even valid for periods ≥144 — with a lookback shorter than one daily cycle, high period levels may be no-ops, which could produce a spurious 'no effect' result.", "Exact ETTm1 file length, channel order, missing-value handling (the published ETT files contain NaNs) and the split constants used by the PDF authors; normalization choice (RevIN vs train standardization) changes reported MSE by more than some plausible period effects.", "Whether published PDF results on ETTm1 exist and under which protocol — needed to know whether our absolute numbers should be comparable to a table or only internally comparable across period levels.", "Unknown target metric direction of interest: best average over horizons vs best at long horizons (period effects are commonly horizon-dependent).", "Compute budget and wall-clock allowance for the real run are unspecified; my 108+ run sizing may exceed what the researcher can spend, and the trim path (2 seeds, horizons {96,720}) would weaken the seed-level variance estimate.", "Storage: / is tmpfs, so run artifacts may vanish unless an export location is designated.", "Dependency availability offline: even with 224 CPUs, no torch/numpy means nothing runs today; provisioning is a hard prerequisite, not a footnote."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-87bdeff6bafe53b0", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-87bdeff6bafe53b0", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"## 1) INSPECTION REPORT \\u2014 the repo is empty. Plainly.\\n\\nWhat exists in /workspace: exactly one file, `task.json` (389 bytes), containing the question, a context string, and a note `\\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"`\\n\\nWhat is ABSENT (all verified, not assumed):\\n- **PDF model code / paper / PDF-related PDFs: none.** No `.py`, no `.ipynb`, no `exp_main.py`, no `models/`, no `configs/`, no docs. Therefore I cannot read the \\\"period\\\" mechanism, cannot see its hyperparameter name (`period`? `patch_len`? `period_len`? `num_periods`?), its default value, or whether it interacts with patching/stride/padding.\\n- **ETTm1 dataset / dataloader: none.** No `ETT-small/`, no `ETTm1.csv`, no `data_provider/`, no `.csv` anywhere. Whole-filesystem search for `*ETT*`, `ETTm1`, `PatchTST`, `time-series-library` returned nothing.\\n- **Prior experiment scripts or results: none.** No `results/`, no `.log`, no `.csv` metrics, no git history (no `.git`).\\n- **Dependencies: none usable.** Python 3.14.4 only. `numpy`, `pandas`, `scipy`, `torch`, `sklearn`, `gluonts` all fail to import. `pip list` is a Debian system Python set (ansible, cryptography, Jinja2, etc.).\\n- **No GPU** (no `/dev/nvidia*`, no `nvidia-smi`). **No network** (DNS resolution fails; pypi unreachable, so we cannot `pip install` or download ETTm1).\\n- Resources that DO exist: 224 CPUs, ~1 TB free space \\u2014 but note `/` is `tmpfs` (RAM-backed, likely ephemeral), so any artifacts need explicit export.\\n\\nConsequence: **every statement below about the model and about \\\"period\\\" is unverified from source.** The design must be written against an explicitly declared model contract, and the first artifact of any real run must be a diff of our contract against the real PDF source.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2) SKELETON APPROACH (not the final design)\\n\\n**2a. Define the factor first \\u2014 \\\"period\\\" is not one thing.** Three distinct mechanisms are commonly called \\\"period\\\" in this literature, and they lead to different experiments:\\n- **(A) Periodic patch alignment / patch-position embedding period** (PatchTST-style: patches the series and uses `period` to index/positional-encode patch tokens so tokens at the same phase of the cycle share position information). Here `period` is a positional/padding hyperparameter, usually in *raw timesteps*, and it interacts with `patch_len`/`stride`.\\n- **(B) Seasonal/periodicity length used for decomposition or periodic feature/embedding** (Autoformer series-decomposition kernel size, FiLM/FEDformer-style periodic prior, FFT-selected dominant periods). Here `period` is the *cycle length* the model is told to expect.\\n- **(C) Period == patch length** (i.e. the knob is really the tokenization granularity, and \\\"period\\\" is a misnomer).\\n\\nI cannot resolve A vs B vs C from the repo. This is the single most consequential open question and I want it answered by the researcher/source before the final design.\\n\\n**2b. Dose the factor on ETTm1's real periodicity scale.** ETTm1 is 10-minute sampled \\u2192 intrinsic daily cycle = **144 steps**, weekly = **1008 steps**. Candidate levels (to be trimmed to ~7\\u20139 in the final design, expressed in raw timesteps and converted to patch units if mechanism A):\\n- `none` (no periodic prior / plain absolute or learned positions) \\u2014 the reference point\\n- harmonics/mistunes of the daily cycle: 12, 24, 48, 72, 96\\n- **144 (daily, expected optimum)** and 216/288\\n- 504, **1008 (weekly)**\\n- `auto/fft` (period estimated from train split, if the model supports it)\\nDeliberately include the *plausible-but-wrong* value 24 (correct for hourly ETTh, off by 6\\u00d7 for 10-minute ETTm1). If period genuinely matters, mis-tuned periods should degrade in a smooth, interpretable way; that is the mechanistic signature we test for, rather than just \\\"which number wins\\\".\\n\\n**2c. Baselines / controls.**\\n1. `none` (no periodic prior) \\u2014 primary control, isolates the effect of having a period at all.\\n2. 144 / 1008 \\\"oracle\\\" periods from ETTm1's known sampling rate.\\n3. Mistranslated-period control (24, and a non-divisor like 100) \\u2014 tests sensitivity/robustness, not just fit.\\n4. Lookback-value (seasonal naive) and, if cheap, a simple decomposition baseline (e.g. DLinear) to anchor absolute numbers and tell us whether period