{"id": "75aa2bbe-7391-44d9-bda0-2a0512913e1c", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:21:20.331967", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "125f55b2-7175-400f-8970-3d7753ac0170", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Two hard findings from the arithmetic that change the grid: at seq_len=96 **four** levels (100/144/288/1008) contain zero complete cycles, and 1008 contains none even at 336; and period=24 is *unrepresentable* when stride=16. Writing the design doc now."}], "reasoning_content": "Key finding: at seq_len=96, period levels ≥ 144 have ZERO complete cycles in the lookback — this is the degenerate regime. p=100 is non-divisor of stride (12.5 patches/period) and 96 doesn't fit... Actually p=100 > 96 lookback so int_periods=0 too. Good: at seq_len=96, only 24, 48, 96 have ≥1 complete cycle; 100, 144, 288, 1008 are all degenerate. That strongly justifies the seq_len=336 arm. Also at 336, 1008 still has 0 complete cycles (336 < 1008) — so the weekly level needs seq_len=1008+ arm or must be flagged as extrapolation-only. Important finding to feed into design: drop 1008 to a conditional/flagged arm, or add a third lookback arm seq_len=1152? That's expensive. Better: define the lookback rule as \"lookback ≥ 2 periods\" for the primary curve, and treat 1008 as a separate flagged arm (out-of-support) with either seq_len=1008/1152 or excluded with rationale. Let me compute a lookback-scaled arm: L_seq = max(96, 2*p) rounded to stride multiples → 48→96, 24→96, 96→192, 100→200, 144→288, 288→576, 1008→2016(too long). Hmm.\n\nBetter design decision: two-factor lookback×period is the honest way, but researcher constrained: seq_len=96 primary, seq_len=336 arm for p≥144. I'll add: for p=1008, seq_len=336 covers only 1/3 cycle → mark as \"outside-support level, reported for curve-monotonicity only, not interpreted as a weekly-period test\"; optional exploratory arm seq_len=1152 for {none, 1008} only if budget allows (cheap: 2 levels × 4 horizons × 3 seeds = 24 runs).\n\nAlso note the exact_phase check: p=24 with L=16,s=16 → non-integer patches/period (1.5) → unrepresentable at patch granularity; so period 24 is unrepresentable under patch_len=stride=16. That's a real representability finding. Also p=100 unrepresentable under all strides tested (12.5, 8.33, 6.25 patches/period) — good, that's intentional (non-divisor control), but must log actual rounded value.\n\nAlso with stride=patch_len (non-overlapping), the number of patches at seq_len=96 is only 12 (L=8) / 8 (L=12) / 6 (L=16) — very few tokens. Fine.\n\nNow the run count. Let me finalize the grid.\n\nFactor levels (primary, 8 levels):\n1. `none` (no periodic prior; absolute/learned positions) — control\n2. 24 (mistranslated-from-hourly, off-by-6)\n3. 48\n4. 96\n5. 100 (non-divisor control)\n6. 144 (daily oracle)\n7. 288\n8. 1008 (weekly oracle — flagged, outside support at both lookbacks)\nOptional 9th: `auto/fft` if supported.\n\nLookback arms: A96 = seq_len 96 (all levels), A336 = seq_len 336 (levels with 2p ≥ 96, i.e. p ≥ 48: 48, 96, 100, 144, 288, 1008 + none → 7 levels). Per researcher: \"for levels period ≥ 144 add arm with seq_len=336\". I'll implement: A336 mandatory for p ∈ {144, 288, 1008} plus none + 48/96/100 anchors for interpretability → 7 levels.\n\nHorizons 4, seeds 3.\n\nPrimary grid: 8 levels × 4 h × 3 seeds = 96.\nLookback arm A336: 7 × 4 × 3 = 84. Hmm, that's bigger than the researcher's ~108 total. Researcher said \"~108 runs + controls, overnight on GPU\" ok as planning estimate, and asked for the seq_len=336 arm for p≥144. Minimal version: {none,144,288,1008} × 4 × 3 = 48. That's the honest minimum to serve its purpose (it's a degenerate-regime resolution arm, not a full re-run). I'll present a Tier-1 (48) and note the optional extended A336 (+36 for 48/96/100).\n\n2-factor period × patch_len {8,12,16} at H=720 only: to avoid double counting the primary's already-included patch_len (primary uses one of them, say L=16 or L=12?), the 2-factor sweep = 8 levels × 3 patch_lens × 1 horizon × 3 seeds = 72, minus the 3 cells already run in primary config → 8×3=24 configs, 1 is primary → 23 new × 3 seeds = 69. Present as: 24 combos × 3 seeds = 72, of which 24 already exist in primary (H=720 subset for the primary patch_len) → +48 new.\n\nControls: seasonal-naive (0 GPU runs, closed form, per channel, 4 horizons), DLinear (optional cheap, 4 h × 3 seeds = 12), PatchTST-ablation conditional on branch A: {none, 24, 96, 144, 1008} × 4 × 3 = 60.\n\nTotals: core 96 + A336 48 = 144 model runs; +2-factor 48 = 192; +DLinear 12 = 204; +conditional A branch 60 = 264. Report as tiers with a minimum viable set (~144 + naive) and full set.\n\nLet me compute a quick sizing note: I cannot measure runtime (no torch). I should be careful: I'll frame GPU-time as an explicit unknown with a placeholder measurement protocol (timed single-run pilot before the sweep) rather than assert overnight. Researcher said \"feasibility sizing (~108 runs + controls, overnight on GPU, CPU-only as risk) ok to leave as planning estimate\" — so I keep it as an explicitly labeled estimate plus a pilot-timing gate.\n\nAlso pre-registration of decision rule and analysis plan.\n\nLet me also compute the seasonal-naive baseline expectation: with period 144 as the naive seasonal cycle. Fine, and note the seasonal-naive MSE is itself a period-dependent baseline → include naive at lag 96, 144, 288, 1008 to show the \"period matters\" effect also exists in a parameter-free model = sanity check that any observed PDF period effect exceeds what trivial period selection buys. Nice addition.\n\nAlso NaN handling: ETTm1 has NaNs in the published file; must be resolved in preflight.\n\nMetrics: MSE/MAE on train-split-standardized OT=HUFL and all 7 channels averaged. Standard convention: standardize per channel with train mean/std, metric computed on standardized scale.\n\nNow the deliverable: write /workspace/period_eptt_m1_design.md (design doc as final deliverable) — but the task says \"no executed experiment\"; writing a design doc file to /workspace is fine and the researcher asked for artifacts in /workspace with tmpfs note. The final deliverable = a single design document. I'll write it to /workspace/period_design.md and then hand off stage=result with the full design in `answer`.\n\nCareful about the budget: researcher wants full design in answer. Let me write the doc file and include the same content in the answer.\n\nLet me write the file.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cd /workspace && cat > period_design.md <<'DOC'\n# Experiment Design — Effect of `period` on PDF model performance, ETTm1\nSTATUS: design-only. NO experiment has been run. NO result below is measured.\n/workspace is tmpfs: this file may not persist across environment restarts.\n\n## 0. Declared model contract (PDF source is ABSENT from /workspace)\nNo PDF code/paper exists in the environment. This design binds to a contract, not to code.\nC1. PDF exposes a configurable hyperparameter `period` settable manually in RAW TIMESTEPS.\nC2. `period` can be disabled -> yields the `none` control arm.\nC3. All other PDF hyperparameters are fixed, identical config strings across arms (diff-checked).\nC4. If C1/C2 fail -> CONTINGENCY: code patch. The factor is then an INTERVENTION ON A PERIOD\n    ESTIMATOR, not a config knob; report as such, never as \"PDF's period setting\".\nMECHANISM BRANCHES (design divergences only):\n  A patch/period-position embedding -> units in raw timesteps; representability via stride;\n    `none` = plain learned positions; PatchTST-no-period ablation APPLIES.\n  B seasonal/decomposition periodic prior -> `none` = no periodic branch; PatchTST ablation N/A.\n  C period IS patch length -> factor degenerates into patch_len sweep; use the 2-factor\n    period x patch_len grid AS the primary analysis; oracle-144 rationale still valid.\nEvery reported result is labelled with which branch the executed code turned out to be.\n\n## 1. Rationale: why period could matter at all on ETTm1\nETTm1 is 10-min sampled => daily cycle = 24*6 = 144 steps, weekly = 1008 steps (arithmetic\nverified: 144/day, 1008/week). ETT load curves are dominated by a diurnal cycle plus a\nweekday/weekend modulation. A periodic prior is useful only if (a) the declared cycle matches\nthe true one and (b) the lookback contains enough whole cycles to align phase to it.\nTherefore the predicted signature is a smooth minimum near 144 (and secondary structure near\n1008), with degradation proportional to phase mismatch: period=24 (correct for HOURLY ETTh,\nwrong by 6x for 10-min ETTm1) should behave poorly, and a non-divisor (100) should behave worse\nthan its neighbours through phase drift. If instead the curve is flat, the mechanism is not\nperiod-sensitive on this dataset - a real, reportable answer.\n\n## 2. Factor and grid\n### 2.1 Levels (8 primary; the 9th conditional)\n| level | period (raw steps) | role |\n|---|---|---|\n| none | disabled | primary control |\n| 24 | 24 | mis-specified control (ETTh hourly value, off by 6x) |\n| 48 | 48 | short-cycle interpolation point |\n| 96 | 96 | short-cycle interpolation point |\n| 100 | 100 | non-divisor control -> phase drift |\n| 144 | 144 | ORACLE daily cycle |\n| 288 | 288 | 2-day, above-oracle |\n| 1008 | 1008 | weekly oracle, OUTSIDE-SUPPORT FLAG (see 2.3) |\n| auto/fft | train-fit estimate | conditional, only if PDF supports it |\n### 2.2 Lookback arms (mandatory, not a caveat)\nDegeneracy computed, not guessed: with seq_len=96, levels 100/144/288/1008 contain\n**zero** whole cycles in the lookback (S//p = 0). At seq_len=336, 144 -> 2 cycles, 288 -> 1\ncycle. So a \"no effect\" at p>=144 under seq_len=96 would be a lookback artifact.\n* ARM-96 (primary): seq_len=96, all 8 levels. Answers \"period under the standard budget\".\n* ARM-336 (required): seq_len=336, levels {none,144,288,1008}. Resolves the degenerate regime.\n  Primary claim about p>=144 comes from ARM-336, not ARM-96.\n  Optional extension (+36 runs): add {48,96,100} to ARM-336 for a same-lookback curve.\n* ARM-1152 (exploratory, optional): seq_len=1152, levels {none,1008} only. Without it the\n  weekly level is untestable (336 < 1008 => 0 whole cycles).\n### 2.3 Representability rule (branch A) and logged artifacts\nLevel is representable iff (period % stride == 0) and (seq_len % stride == 0); otherwise the\neffective period is round(period/stride)*stride and BOTH target and effective values are logged.\nMeasured conflicts: 100 is unrepresentable at every stride tested (8/12/16 -> 12.5, 8.33, 6.25\npatches per cycle) - intentional, it is the phase-drift control, but it is NOT a clean level;\n24 is unrepresentable at stride=16 (1.5 patches/cycle) and is therefore DROPPED from the\n2-factor cell (L=16,s=16); seq_len=96 with L=16,s=8 gives 11 patches, so short periods give\nvery few tokens per cycle. Token-count per cycle is reported per cell because low counts are a\ncompeting explanation for degradation.\n### 2.4 Fixed context (declared convention, not author defaults - those are unknown)\nmultivariate, all 7 channels; MSE/MAE averaged over channels; horizons 96/192/336/720;\none model per horizon; patch_len=16, stride=8 unless the preflight shows PDF defaults differ\n(preflight defaults win over this line); batch/optim/epochs/dropout/d_model fixed and logged;\nseed set {0,1,2}.\n\n## 3. Controls and baselines\n1. `none` - the counterfactual. Without it, a \"best period\" says nothing about whether the\n   periodic prior helps.\n2. Oracle 144/1008 vs mis-specified 24 - mechanistic signature test.\n3. Non-divisor 100 - separates \"wrong cycle length\" from \"phase cannot be represented\".\n4. Seasonal naive (mandatory, zero-GPU, closed form) at lags 96/144/288/1008. A parameter-free\n   model also has a \"period\"; if PDF's best-vs-worst gap does not exceed the naive's own\n   lag-sensitivity, the period effect is not model-specific signal.\n5. DLinear (cheap, optional) - architecture-free anchor at the same splits.\n6. PatchTST-no-period ablation - BRANCH A ONLY; separates period-effect from PDF-specific\n   machinery. N/A under branch B; becomes the primary analysis under branch C.\n\n## 4. Data protocol\nETTm1, 10-min, ~69,680 rows x 7 channels (row count to be CONFIRMED at preflight).\nSplit (standard Informer/Autoformer 12/2/~4 convention, computed here): train [0, 51840);\nval [51552, 60480); test [60192, 69680) where the 288-step left shift on val/test = seq_len\nheadroom and 69680 clamps the nominal 73440. Usable test windows: 8673 at seq_len=96/H=720;\n8433 at seq_len=336/H=720. Borders must be re-derived from the actual dataloader at preflight;\nshift = lookback + horizon so no test window is lost.\nNormalization: per-channel standardization using TRAIN-only mean/std; MSE/MAE on the\nstandardized scale (published-convention comparable), plus denormalized MAE for interpretability.\nNaNs: published ETTm1 contains NaNs. Policy fixed before any run: drop any window overlapping a\nNaN row (reported), never interpolate across the train/val/test boundary.\nLeakage: no statistic (mean, std, FFT period, best epoch, best checkpoint) may touch val+test;\nFFT/auto-period fit on train only. Checkpoint selection on val MSE only; test touched once,\nafter the analysis plan is frozen.\n\n## 5. Analysis plan (pre-registered; freeze before test access)\nPrimary statistic per cell: mean +/- sample SD of test MSE over 3 seeds (also MAE).\nDECISION RULES, fixed in advance:\nR1 A period effect is CLAIMED for level p vs none iff |MSE(p)-MSE(none)| > max(SD_p, SD_none)\n   at >=3 of the 4 horizons in the same arm (same arm required: cross-arm deltas are not used).\nR2 The oracle claim additionally requires MSE(144) < MSE(24) AND MSE(144) < MSE(100) at >=3\n   horizons. Otherwise: monotonicity not established.\nR3 A flat-curve conclusion requires the observed range across levels to be < max SD across\n   levels AND to be smaller than the seasonal-naive lag range. \"No effect\" is only admissible\n   against that yardstick.\nR4 Model selection uses ARM-96 only. ARM-336/1152 are pre-registered sensitivity analyses and\n   cannot be used to pick a period.\nOUTPUT TABLES (both required, they answer different questions):\n  T1 effect curve at fixed shared settings: rows = period levels, cols = horizons x arm,\n     cells = mean MSE +/- SD, MAE, token/cycle count, effective period.\n  T2 best-on-val period per horizon (with its val MSE), showing whether val selection recovers\n     the test-optimal level - a per-horizon level disagreement is itself a finding.\nT3 = T1 restricted to branch A: regress log MSE gap on |log(p/144)| and on tokens-per-cycle to\nattribute degradation between phase mismatch and token starvation.\n\n## 6. Confound policy\nMain sweep: ONLY `period` varies; a single shared config string, diffed between arms.\n2-FActor sweep (branch A, horizon 720 only): period x patch_len {8,12,16} (stride=patch_len),\n8 x 3 = 24 combos x 3 seeds = 72 runs, of which 24 already exist in ARM-96 -> +48 new. Purpose:\ntest whether apparent period effects are patch-granularity effects in disguise.\nLookback is the dominant confound and is handled by design (arm structure), not by discussion.\nReported n, seeds and effective period accompany every number; single-seed cells are not\npooled with 3-seed cells.\n\n## 7. Run budget and feasibility\nTier 1 (minimum defensible): ARM-96 8x4x3 = 96 + ARM-336 4x4x3 = 48 = 144 model runs\n+ seasonal naive (0 GPU).\nTier 2: + 2-factor 48 + DLinear 12 = 204 total.\nTier 3: + ARM-1152 24 + extended ARM-336 36 + PatchTST ablation 60 = 324.\nCPU pilot to measure nothing (no framework installed). GPU wall-clock is an ESTIMATE ONLY,\nflagged as unmeasured: small-patcher on ETTm1 is typically minutes/run, so Tier 1 ~ overnight\nand Tier 2 ~ a day on one GPU. RISK: no GPU in this environment; CPU-only execution is a\nseparate, unvalidated path. TRIM ORDER if budget shrinks: seeds 3->2 (weakens R1, must be\nstated), Tier-3 items, ARM-336 extension, DLinear. Never trim ARM-336 or the naive baseline.\nCurrent environment cannot run ANY of this (see 8): the deliverable is the design.\n\n## 8. Provisioning preflight (HARD PREREQUISITES - nothing runs until all pass)\nP1 PDF source obtained; `period` name, units, default, and its interaction with patch_len/\n   stride read from code; contract C1-C4 and branch A/B/C RECONCILED; any divergence recorded\n   as a design amendment BEFORE running.\nP2 ETTm1 CSV obtained; row count, channel count/names, sampling interval, NaN counts verified;\n   split borders taken from the actual dataloader and compared to the computed values in S4.\nP3 Framework + deps installed (torch/numpy/pandas currently MISSING); one smoke run per arm\n   completes; peak memory at seq_len=336 measured (weekly arm memory risk).\nP4 Analysis script validated on synthetic data; decision rules R1-R4 and T1-T3 frozen;\n   val-only selection hook verified.\nP5 Artifacts written to /workspace (tmpfs - copy out at the end); per-run config hash + log\n   so every number is traceable.\n\n## 9. Limitations / threats to validity\n* PDF identity and architecture UNKNOWN: no source in /workspace. Results are conditional on\n  the declared contract; a different mechanism (A/B/C) changes interpretation.\n* `period` units (raw steps vs patches vs multiplier) unverified -> all reported values carry\n  both target and effective values; a units error is the top threat to the whole curve.\n* seq_len=96 cannot hold one weekly cycle: the weekly claim needs ARM-1152, otherwise 1008 is\n  reported as outside-support, not as \"weekly period does not help\".\n* 3 seeds is thin for SD-based rules (R1/R3 are conservative, not powerful); with 2 seeds the\n  rules must be downgraded to descriptive.\n* External ETT tables are largely single-seed and heterogeneous in split/normalization:\n  comparisons are INTERNAL only; cross-paper numbers are not validity evidence.\n* Fixed other-hyperparameters understates the best achievable period (no co-tuning) - by\n  design; the 2-factor sweep bounds but does not eliminate this.\n* 12/2/4-month split is a single train/test regime: one seasonal transition; period estimates\n  could be season-specific. Out of scope, named as a limitation.\n* ETTm1 is one dataset; generality across ETTh1/ETTm2/traffic is not claimed.\n* /workspace is tmpfs: unexported artifacts can vanish.\nDOC\nwc -l period_design.md && ls -la", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b8f84595629bbec1", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-b8f84595629bbec1", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /workspace && cat > period_design.md <<'DOC'\\n# Experiment Design \\u2014 Effect of `period` on PDF model performance, ETTm1\\nSTATUS: design-only. NO experiment has been run. NO result below is measured.\\n/workspace is tmpfs: this file may not persist across environment restarts.\\n\\n## 0. Declared model contract (PDF source is ABSENT from /workspace)\\nNo PDF code/paper exists in the environment. This design binds to a contract, not to code.\\nC1. PDF exposes a configurable hyperparameter `period` settable manually in RAW TIMESTEPS.\\nC2. `period` can be disabled -> yields the `none` control arm.\\nC3. All other PDF hyperparameters are fixed, identical config strings across arms (diff-checked).\\nC4. If C1/C2 fail -> CONTINGENCY: code patch. The factor is then an INTERVENTION ON A PERIOD\\n    ESTIMATOR, not a config knob; report as such, never as \\\"PDF's period setting\\\".\\nMECHANISM BRANCHES (design divergences only):\\n  A patch/period-position embedding -> units in raw timesteps; representability via stride;\\n    `none` = plain learned positions; PatchTST-no-period ablation APPLIES.\\n  B seasonal/decomposition periodic prior -> `none` = no periodic branch; PatchTST ablation N/A.\\n  C period IS patch length -> factor degenerates into patch_len sweep; use the 2-factor\\n    period x patch_len grid AS the primary analysis; oracle-144 rationale still valid.\\nEvery reported result is labelled with which branch the executed code turned out to be.\\n\\n## 1. Rationale: why period could matter at all on ETTm1\\nETTm1 is 10-min sampled => daily cycle = 24*6 = 144 steps, weekly = 1008 steps (arithmetic\\nverified: 144/day, 1008/week). ETT load curves are dominated by a diurnal cycle plus a\\nweekday/weekend modulation. A periodic prior is useful only if (a) the declared cycle matches\\nthe true one and (b) the lookback contains enough whole cycles to align phase to it.\\nTherefore the predicted signature is a smooth minimum near 144 (and secondary structure near\\n1008), with degradation proportional to phase mismatch: period=24 (correct for HOURLY ETTh,\\nwrong by 6x for 10-min ETTm1) should behave poorly, and a non-divisor (100) should behave worse\\nthan its neighbours through phase drift. If instead the curve is flat, the mechanism is not\\nperiod-sensitive on this dataset - a real, reportable answer.\\n\\n## 2. Factor and grid\\n### 2.1 Levels (8 primary; the 9th conditional)\\n| level | period (raw steps) | role |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| none | disabled | primary control |\\n| 24 | 24 | mis-specified control (ETTh hourly value, off by 6x) |\\n| 48 | 48 | short-cycle interpolation point |\\n| 96 | 96 | short-cycle interpolation point |\\n| 100 | 100 | non-divisor control -> phase drift |\\n| 144 | 144 | ORACLE daily cycle |\\n| 288 | 288 | 2-day, above-oracle |\\n| 1008 | 1008 | weekly oracle, OUTSIDE-SUPPORT FLAG (see 2.3) |\\n| auto/fft | train-fit estimate | conditional, only if PDF supports it |\\n### 2.2 Lookback arms (mandatory, not a caveat)\\nDegeneracy computed, not guessed: with seq_len=96, levels 100/144/288/1008 contain\\n**zero** whole cycles in the lookback (S//p = 0). At seq_len=336, 144 -> 2 cycles, 288 -> 1\\ncycle. So a \\\"no effect\\\" at p>=144 under seq_len=96 would be a lookback artifact.\\n* ARM-96 (primary): seq_len=96, all 8 levels. Answers \\\"period under the standard budget\\\".\\n* ARM-336 (required): seq_len=336, levels {none,144,288,1008}. Resolves the degenerate regime.\\n  Primary claim about p>=144 comes from ARM-336, not ARM-96.\\n  Optional extension (+36 runs): add {48,96,100} to ARM-336 for a same-lookback curve.\\n* ARM-1152 (exploratory, optional): seq_len=1152, levels {none,1008} only. Without it the\\n  weekly level is untestable (336 < 1008 => 0 whole cycles).\\n### 2.3 Representability rule (branch A) and logged artifacts\\nLevel is representable iff (period % stride == 0) and (seq_len % stride == 0); otherwise the\\neffective period is round(period/stride)*stride and BOTH target and effective values are logged.\\nMeasured conflicts: 100 is unrepresentable at every stride tested (8/12/16 -> 12.5, 8.33, 6.25\\npatches per cycle) - intentional, it is the phase-drift control, but it is NOT a clean level;\\n24 is unrepresentable at stride=16 (1.5 patches/cycle) and is therefore DROPPED from the\\n2-factor cell (L=16,s=16); seq_len=96 with L=16,s=8 gives 11 patches, so short periods give\\nvery few tokens per cycle. Token-count per cycle is reported per cell because low counts are a\\ncompeting explanation for degradation.\\n### 2.4 Fixed context (declared convention, not author defaults - those are unknown)\\nmultivariate, all 7 channels; MSE/MAE averaged over channels; horizons 96/192/336/720;\\none model per horizon; patch_len=16, stride=8 unless the preflight shows PDF defaults differ\\n(preflight defaults win over this line); batch/optim/epochs/dropout/d_model fixed and logged;\\nseed set {0,1,2}.\\n\\n## 3. Controls and baselines\\n1. `none` - the counterfactual. Without it, a \\\"best period\\\" says nothing about whether the\\n   periodic prior helps.\\n2. Oracle 144/1008 vs mis-specified 24 - mechanistic signature test.\\n3. Non-divisor 100 - separates \\\"wrong cycle length\\\" from \\\"phase cannot be represented\\\".\\n4. Seasonal naive (mandatory, zero-GPU, closed form) at lags 96/144/288/1008. A parameter-free\\n   model also has a \\\"period\\\"; if PDF's best-vs-worst gap does not exceed the naive's own\\n   lag-sensitivity, the period effect is not model-specific signal.\\n5. DLinear (cheap, optional) - architecture-free anchor at the same splits.\\n6. PatchTST-no-period ablation - BRANCH A ONLY; separates period-effect from PDF-specific\\n   machinery. N/A under branch B; becomes the primary analysis under branch C.\\n\\n## 4. Data protocol\\nETTm1, 10-min, ~69,680 rows x 7 channels (row count to be CONFIRMED at preflight).\\nSplit (standard Informer/Autoformer 12/2/~4 convention, computed here): train [0, 51840);\\nval [51552, 60480); test [60192, 69680) where the 288-step left shift on val/test = seq_len\\nheadroom and 69680 clamps the nominal 73440. Usable test windows: 8673 at seq_len=96/H=720;\\n8433 at seq_len=336/H=720. Borders must be re-derived from the actual dataloader at preflight;\\nshift = lookback + horizon so no test window is lost.\\nNormalization: per-channel standardization using TRAIN-only mean/std; MSE/MAE on the\\nstandardized scale (published-convention comparable), plus denormalized MAE for interpretability.\\nNaNs: published ETTm1 contains NaNs. Policy fixed before any run: drop any window overlapping a\\nNaN row (reported), never interpolate across the train/val/test boundary.\\nLeakage: no statistic (mean, std, FFT period, best epoch, best checkpoint) may touch val+test;\\nFFT/auto-period fit on train only. Checkpoint selection on val MSE only; test touched once,\\nafter the analysis plan is frozen.\\n\\n## 5. Analysis plan (pre-registered; freeze before test access)\\nPrimary statistic per cell: mean +/- sample SD of test MSE over 3 seeds (also MAE).\\nDECISION RULES, fixed in advance:\\nR1 A period effect is CLAIMED for level p vs none iff |MSE(p)-MSE(none)| > max(SD_p, SD_none)\\n   at >=3 of the 4 horizons in the same arm (same arm required: cross-arm deltas are not used).\\nR2 The oracle claim additionally requires MSE(144) < MSE(24) AND MSE(144) < MSE(100) at >=3\\n   horizons. Otherwise: monotonicity not established.\\nR3 A flat-curve conclusion requires the observed range across levels to be < max SD across\\n   levels AND to be smaller than the seasonal-naive lag range. \\\"No effect\\\" is only admissible\\n   against that yardstick.\\nR4 Model selection uses ARM-96 only. ARM-336/1152 are pre-registered sensitivity analyses and\\n   cannot be used to pick a period.\\nOUTPUT TABLES (both required, they answer different questions):\\n  T1 effect curve at fixed shared settings: rows = period levels, cols = horizons x arm,\\n     cells = mean MSE +/- SD, MAE, token/cycle count, effective period.\\n  T2 best-on-val period per horizon (with its val MSE), showing whether val selection recovers\\n     the test-optimal level - a per-horizon level disagreement is itself a finding.\\nT3 = T1 restricted to branch A: regress log MSE gap on |log(p/144)| and on tokens-per-cycle to\\nattribute degradation between phase mismatch and token starvation.\\n\\n## 6. Confound policy\\nMain sweep: ONLY `period` varies; a single shared config string, diffed between arms.\\n2-FActor sweep (branch A, horizon 720 only): period x patch_len {8,12,16} (stride=patch_len),\\n8 x 3 = 24 combos x 3 seeds = 72 runs, of which 24 already exist in ARM-96 -> +48 new. Purpose:\\ntest whether apparent period effects are patch-granularity effects in disguise.\\nLookback is the dominant confound and is handled by design (arm structure), not by discussion.\\nReported n, seeds and effective period accompany every number; single-seed cells are not\\npooled with 3-seed cells.\\n\\n## 7. Run budget and feasibility\\nTier 1 (minimum defensible): ARM-96 8x4x3 = 96 + ARM-336 4x4x3 = 48 = 144 model runs\\n+ seasonal naive (0 GPU).\\nTier 2: + 2-factor 48 + DLinear 12 = 204 total.\\nTier 3: + ARM-1152 24 + extended ARM-336 36 + PatchTST ablation 60 = 324.\\nCPU pilot to measure nothing (no framework installed). GPU wall-clock is an ESTIMATE ONLY,\\nflagged as unmeasured: small-patcher on ETTm1 is typically minutes/run, so Tier 1 ~ overnight\\nand Tier 2 ~ a day on one GPU. RISK: no GPU in this environment; CPU-only execution is a\\nseparate, unvalidated path. TRIM ORDER if budget shrinks: seeds 3->2 (weakens R1, must be\\nstated), Tier-3 items, ARM-336 extension, DLinear. Never trim ARM-336 or the naive baseline.\\nCurrent environment cannot run ANY of this (see 8): the deliverable is the design.\\n\\n## 8. Provisioning preflight (HARD PREREQUISITES - nothing runs until all pass)\\nP1 PDF source obtained; `period` name, units, default, and its interaction with patch_len/\\n   stride read from code; contract C1-C4 and branch A/B/C RECONCILED; any divergence recorded\\n   as a design amendment BEFORE running.\\nP2 ETTm1 CSV obtained; row count, channel count/names, sampling interval, NaN counts verified;\\n   split borders taken from the actual dataloader and compared to the computed values in S4.\\nP3 Framework + deps installed (torch/numpy/pandas currently MISSING); one smoke run per arm\\n   completes; peak memory at seq_len=336 measured (weekly arm memory risk).\\nP4 Analysis script validated on synthetic data; decision rules R1-R4 and T1-T3 frozen;\\n   val-only selection hook verified.\\nP5 Artifacts written to /workspace (tmpfs - copy out at the end); per-run config hash + log\\n   so every number is traceable.\\n\\n## 9. Limitations / threats to validity\\n* PDF identity and architecture UNKNOWN: no source in /workspace. Results are conditional on\\n  the declared contract; a different mechanism (A/B/C) changes interpretation.\\n* `period` units (raw steps vs patches vs multiplier) unverified -> all reported values carry\\n  both target and effective values; a units error is the top threat to the whole curve.\\n* seq_len=96 cannot hold one weekly cycle: the weekly claim needs ARM-1152, otherwise 1008 is\\n  reported as outside-support, not as \\\"weekly period does not help\\\".\\n* 3 seeds is thin for SD-based rules (R1/R3 are conservative, not powerful); with 2 seeds the\\n  rules must be downgraded to descriptive.\\n* External ETT tables are largely single-seed and heterogeneous in split/normalization:\\n  comparisons are INTERNAL only; cross-paper numbers are not validity evidence.\\n* Fixed other-hyperparameters understates the best achievable period (no co-tuning) - by\\n  design; the 2-factor sweep bounds but does not eliminate this.\\n* 12/2/4-month split is a single train/test regime: one seasonal transition; period estimates\\n  could be season-specific. Out of scope, named as a limitation.\\n* ETTm1 is one dataset; generality across ETTh1/ETTm2/traffic is not claimed.\\n* /workspace is tmpfs: unexported artifacts can vanish.\\nDOC\\nwc -l period_design.md && ls -la\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-1f4b2045-a6d6-4bc0-8c85-d590414deb75", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "execute_check: {\"command\": \"cd /workspace && cat > period_design.md <<'DOC'\\n# Experiment Design \\u2014 Effect of `period` on PDF model performance, ETTm1\\nSTATUS: design-only. NO experiment has been run. NO result below is measured.\\n/workspace is tmpfs: this file may not persist across environment restarts.\\n\\n## 0. Declared model contract (PDF source is ABSENT from /workspace)\\nNo PDF code/paper exists in the environment. This design binds to a contract, not to code.\\nC1. PDF exposes a configurable hyperparameter `period` settable manually in RAW TIMESTEPS.\\nC2. `period` can be disabled -> yields the `none` control arm.\\nC3. All other PDF hyperparameters are fixed, identical config strings across arms (diff-checked).\\nC4. If C1/C2 fail -> CONTINGENCY: code patch. The factor is then an INTERVENTION ON A PERIOD\\n    ESTIMATOR, not a config knob; repo